{
  "message_id": "logistics-ops-01",
  "speech": "My initial operational interpretation is that we have a handoff risk to address alongside an uncertain change in reported exceptions. The movement from 11% to 17% does not, by itself, tell me whether workers need more training or whether AI made handling worse. Analyst, what exactly counts as an exception in each period, and are the denominators and recording rules comparable? I would like the audit to distinguish depots and overnight versus other shifts before we target practice.\n\nMy own evidence is that supervisors have no agreed overnight override owner (L-O1). Only 15 minutes per worker are available this week, with no full-shift shutdown (L-O2). A senior dispatcher can staff one two-hour exception clinic, but cannot cover every shift (L-O3). That makes universal clinic attendance unrealistic. I propose we first define an override handoff with an explicitly accepted owner and backup on each affected shift; coverage still needs checking before I commit to its implementation. Any practice and checking must share the 15-minute limit.\n\nI suggest provisionally reserving 2 units for workflow, 2 for your segmented audit, and 1 for learning-scientist advice, leaving 5 units pending that advice. I cannot certify assessment validity. I would ask the scientist what a six-item check could reasonably support, and how to fit practice plus a changed-case check within our time limit. Would your audit reveal which exception types and shifts should receive that scarce practice time, or is recording quality itself the first constraint?",
  "evidence_disclosed": [
    "L-O1",
    "L-O2",
    "L-O3"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Overnight override ownership is currently unagreed.",
      "basis": [
        "L-O1"
      ],
      "competency": "shift workflow"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Practice and checking together must fit 15 minutes per worker; one two-hour clinic cannot provide coverage across every shift.",
      "basis": [
        "L-O2",
        "L-O3"
      ],
      "competency": "staffing and workplace-training implementation"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The reported exception-rate change alone is insufficient for an operational decision to attribute the problem to training.",
      "basis": [
        "public.brief"
      ],
      "competency": "basic descriptive measurement; causal attribution explicitly unresolved"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The provisional workflow, audit, and consultation reservation totals 5 of 10 units.",
      "basis": [
        "public.budget",
        "public.cost_reference"
      ],
      "competency": "workplace implementation resource planning"
    }
  ],
  "consult_request": {
    "role": "scientist",
    "question": "What conclusions could a six-item practice check reasonably support about independent competence, and what feasible practice plus changed-case check would you advise within a total of 15 minutes per worker? Advice costs 1 unit; operational coverage remains my responsibility."
  },
  "decision": "proposal"
}
{
  "message_id": "logistics-analyst-01",
  "speech": "Ops, I would first separate the reported increase in exceptions from a demonstrated deterioration in dispatch work. My export documentation shows that rollout changed the denominator from completed jobs to all attempted jobs (L-D1). The 11% and 17% figures therefore are not directly comparable; they do not establish either an AI effect or a training deficit. I propose a scoped, two-unit shift-level audit to establish what comparable counts the export actually supports, report denominators explicitly, and distinguish observed exceptions from gaps in documentation.\n\nThere is a substantial measurement limit: 38% of night-shift overrides lack annotations, and the available export cannot recover them (L-D2, L-D3). I can describe that missingness by shift, but cannot infer why those overrides happened or treat missing annotations as successful handling. We should preserve an explicit unknown category.\n\nWe also have no delayed unassisted assessment (L-D2). Whether six practice items support a claim about independent competence is outside my assessment-validity scope. I request the learning scientist's one-unit consultation: what, if anything, could an immediate six-item check establish, and what bounded practice and changed-case observation would be defensible within this week without claiming certification?\n\nBefore selecting the workflow intervention, what exception types create the greatest operational consequences, and who currently owns an unresolved exception across shift handoff? Your answer could change which cases we monitor and whether the immediate remedy is ownership, information access, or practice. I can specify monitoring; you retain operational release authority.",
  "evidence_disclosed": [
    "L-D1",
    "L-D2",
    "L-D3"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The reported pre/post exception percentages are not directly comparable because rollout changed their denominator.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D1"
      ],
      "competency": "denominator auditing"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Night-shift override interpretation is limited by 38% missing annotations that the export cannot recover.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D2",
        "L-D3"
      ],
      "competency": "missing-data analysis"
    },
    {
      "claim": "A shift-level audit can describe supported counts and missingness but cannot recover reasons for unannotated overrides.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D3"
      ],
      "competency": "descriptive segmentation and workflow measurement"
    },
    {
      "claim": "No delayed unassisted assessment exists; the meaning of the six-item check requires assessment-specialist advice.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D2",
        "public.roster.analyst",
        "public.roster.scientist"
      ],
      "competency": "qualified boundary statement; assessment validity outside assigned scope"
    }
  ],
  "consult_request": {
    "role": "scientist",
    "question": "What, if anything, could an immediate six-item practice check establish, and what bounded practice and changed-case observation would be defensible within one week without claiming certification?",
    "cost": 1
  },
  "decision": "proposal"
}
